it's more than three months since  richard corrigay's body was found  the circumstances around his  disappearance and death still aren't  known  but as police continue their  investigation they too are under the  spotlight  and the probe into how the met first  handled the case has now seen  two members of staff served with  misconduct notices  for potentially failing to pass on new  and relevant  information the internal investigation  was launched after complaints made by  the 19 year old's mother  who accused the met of not treating her  seriously when she reported her son  missing  her son disappeared after leaving home  in west london on march the 22nd  the student who had sickle cell disease  was last seen on cctv that night in  laoton and essex  following an extensive police search his  body was found two weeks later  in a pond in epping forest nearby  richard's mother contacted the police  the day after he vanished but he wasn't  officially recorded as missing until 24  hours later  reacting to the misconduct notices she  said it is encouraging to hear that they  are doing something  because i kept calling and at one point  a police officer told me to stop  this news provides some vindication of  what i said all along  about how slow the police were to act  misconduct notices don't necessarily  mean disciplinary action will follow  but this is a welcome step for a family  that's still searching for answers  iva bennett sky news
 

